      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: scope ACP search path approvals [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nAddresses a problem where users running the ACP client could have a path-bearing `search` request treated the same as a query-only search request, even when that path was outside the active working directory.\n\nAI-assisted change.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe ACP permission classifier now applies the same working-directory containment check to path-bearing `search` alias requests that it already applies to file reads. Query-only `search` calls and OpenClaw-owned `web_search` / `memory_search` calls continue to use the read-only search approval path.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUsers keep the low-friction behavior for normal ACP search queries, while `search` calls that target paths outside the current workspace now require the same explicit operator approval as other out-of-scope file access.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- AWS Crabbox focused test run: `run_b1d3f54ab99b` on lease `cbx_2dc5120eeff7` / `quick-lobster`.\n- Command: `corepack pnpm test src/acp/approval-classifier.test.ts src/acp/client.test.ts`.\n- Result: 2 test files passed; 66 tests passed.\n",
        "branch": "fix/fix-847",
